WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.383
Lesson 1.2: Beat Making and Loop-Based Production.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.398
GENAI 101 - Module 1: Music.

3
00:00:08.398 --> 00:00:14.857
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.457 --> 00:00:20.621
In this lesson you'll build a sixteen-bar beat in a browser-
based workstation.

5
00:00:20.621 --> 00:00:31.742
You'll break the beat into layers, write each layer as a
repeating pattern, extend it to sixteen bars with deliberate
variations, and use one of the tool's AI features.

6
00:00:31.742 --> 00:00:37.633
Then you'll share the beat and give a classmate structured
feedback on their arrangement.

7
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:44.387
Computational thinking has four moves, and a drum loop uses
all four at once.

8
00:00:44.387 --> 00:00:50.700
Decomposition: you break the groove into kick, snare, hi-
hat, bass, and chords.

9
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:59.090
Pattern recognition: you notice the hi-hat repeats every
eighth note and the snare lands on two and four.

10
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.406
Abstraction: you reduce the groove to a grid of cells.

11
00:01:03.406 --> 00:01:10.438
And algorithm design: you sequence the bars into a form with
an ordered list of changes.

12
00:01:11.038 --> 00:01:17.478
Loop-based tools show time as a grid: bars across the top,
instruments down the side.

13
00:01:17.478 --> 00:01:20.433
One bar of four-four has sixteen cells.

14
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:32.101
Put the kick on cells one and nine, the snare on five and
thirteen, and a hi-hat on every odd cell, and you have the
most common pattern in popular music.

15
00:01:32.101 --> 00:01:37.632
Almost everything else you've ever danced to is a variation
on that grid.

16
00:01:37.632 --> 00:01:42.253
Write your pattern on paper first; that's your
decomposition.

17
00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:46.331
A sixteen-bar beat is not sixteen different bars.

18
00:01:46.331 --> 00:02:01.590
It's usually one four-bar phrase repeated four times with
small changes: a fill at the end of bar four, the bass
dropping out in bar eight, a new element arriving at bar
nine, a one-beat silence before bar thirteen.

19
00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.771
Listeners hear the repetition as stability and the variation
as interest.

20
00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:10.604
Your numbered list of those changes is your algorithm.

21
00:02:11.204 --> 00:02:14.596
Modern workstations hide AI in several places.

22
00:02:14.596 --> 00:02:19.242
Loop recommendation suggests loops that fit your key and
tempo.

23
00:02:19.242 --> 00:02:23.740
Pattern generation proposes a drum pattern in a chosen
style.

24
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.354
Automatic mastering balances levels and loudness.

25
00:02:27.354 --> 00:02:30.378
Each of these is a decision made for you.

26
00:02:30.378 --> 00:02:40.112
Your job in this lesson is to use at least one, listen with
it and without it, notice what it decided, and decide
whether you agree.

27
00:02:40.112 --> 00:02:43.799
Write that down; it's the heart of your rationale.

28
00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:55.746
When you're done, export the beat, post it with your pattern
grid and your list of variations, and reply to two peers
using the feedback frame in the lesson page.
